{
 "format": "openchronology",
 "version": 1,
 "title": "The United States Marine Corps: A History",
 "description": "From two battalions raised by the Continental Congress in 1775 to the Corps of today: the wars and battles the Marines fought, the leaders who shaped them, and the traditions they carry.\n\n**How to read it.** Wars and campaigns are durations, with a picture behind the timeline while they last; battles, milestones and people are moments with their picture. Chapter headings mark the eras. Hover over a cluster of moments to fan it out, and open any moment for its full notes and sources. Related events (a leader and their battle, a battle and its war) are linked.\n\n**Sources and images.** Each event links the Wikipedia article it draws on. Images are linked, not copied, from Wikimedia Commons, where most are US government works in the public domain; open an image's original for its author and license. Dates are given in UTC; where only the month or year is known, the notes say so.",
 "presentation": {
  "mode": "gregorian",
  "unit": "",
  "scale": "1/1",
  "origin": "0/1",
  "version": 1,
  "offsetMinutes": 0,
  "significantDigits": 6
 },
 "plugins": [
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "moment-icons",
    "name": "Moment icons",
    "fields": [
     {
      "kind": "image-url",
      "label": "Moment icon URL",
      "metadataKey": "iconUrl"
     }
    ],
    "marker": {
     "kind": "image",
     "metadataKey": "iconUrl"
    },
    "source": "function render(moment: string, api: PluginAPI): string { return api.icon(\"iconUrl\"); }",
    "version": 2,
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Show a circular image on each moment, enlarge it on hover, and edit its image URL in the details panel."
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "duration-backgrounds",
    "name": "Duration backgrounds",
    "fields": [
     {
      "kind": "image-url",
      "label": "Background image URL",
      "metadataKey": "backgroundUrl"
     }
    ],
    "targets": [
     "durations"
    ],
    "version": 1,
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "background": {
     "kind": "image",
     "metadataKey": "backgroundUrl"
    },
    "description": "Show an image behind the timeline where a duration lies, centered between its visible start and end and filling the timeline. Smaller durations show above larger ones, and edges blend into each other."
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "expand-on-hover",
    "name": "Expand on hover",
    "fields": [],
    "source": "function render(moment: string, api: PluginAPI): string { return api.expand(); }",
    "summary": {
     "kind": "radial"
    },
    "version": 1,
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Hover or focus a summary of two to five moments to fan out its members radially for individual inspection. Larger summaries stay collapsed."
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "rich-text-notes",
    "name": "Rich text notes",
    "notes": {
     "kind": "markdown"
    },
    "fields": [],
    "source": "function render(moment: string, api: PluginAPI): string { return api.none(); }",
    "version": 1,
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Edit moment and stack notes visually, storing portable Markdown. Hover cards render safe headings, emphasis, lists, code and HTTPS links. HTML and images are not executed."
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "moment-headings",
    "name": "Headings",
    "fields": [
     {
      "kind": "heading",
      "label": "Heading level",
      "metadataKey": "headingLevel"
     },
     {
      "kind": "font",
      "label": "Heading font",
      "metadataKey": "headingFont"
     }
    ],
    "marker": {
     "kind": "heading",
     "metadataKey": "headingLevel"
    },
    "targets": [
     "moments"
    ],
    "version": 1,
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Let a moment act as a heading or marker: only its title shows, sized as a heading from level 1 to 6, and the title is what you click. Choose its font (Times New Roman by default). Headings still group into summaries with other moments."
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "moment-sources",
    "name": "Sources",
    "fields": [
     {
      "kind": "links",
      "label": "Sources",
      "metadataKey": "sources"
     }
    ],
    "source": "function render(moment: string, api: PluginAPI): string { return api.none(); }",
    "version": 1,
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Show and edit a series of source links for moments and stack entries."
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "timeline-background",
    "name": "Timeline background",
    "fields": [],
    "version": 1,
    "backdrop": {
     "kind": "image",
     "setting": "imageUrl"
    },
    "settings": [
     {
      "key": "imageUrl",
      "kind": "image-url",
      "label": "Background image URL"
     }
    ],
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Show one image behind the whole timeline, beneath any duration backgrounds. Set its address here in the plugin list: it belongs to the whole timeline, not to any one event."
   },
   "settings": {
    "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/MARFORK_250th_Marine_Corps_Birthday_Ball_%289415007%29.jpg/1920px-MARFORK_250th_Marine_Corps_Birthday_Ball_%289415007%29.jpg"
   }
  },
  {
   "enabled": true,
   "manifest": {
    "id": "timeline-theme",
    "name": "Timeline theme",
    "theme": {
     "halo": "haloColor",
     "text": "textColor",
     "marker": "markerColor",
     "measure": "measureColor",
     "duration": "durationColor",
     "textFont": "textFont",
     "background": "backgroundColor",
     "measureFont": "measureFont"
    },
    "fields": [],
    "version": 1,
    "settings": [
     {
      "key": "textColor",
      "kind": "color",
      "label": "Label color"
     },
     {
      "key": "textFont",
      "kind": "font",
      "label": "Label font"
     },
     {
      "key": "measureColor",
      "kind": "color",
      "label": "Measure color"
     },
     {
      "key": "measureFont",
      "kind": "font",
      "label": "Measure font"
     },
     {
      "key": "haloColor",
      "kind": "color",
      "label": "Halo color"
     },
     {
      "key": "markerColor",
      "kind": "color",
      "label": "Marker color"
     },
     {
      "key": "durationColor",
      "kind": "color",
      "label": "Duration color"
     },
     {
      "key": "backgroundColor",
      "kind": "color",
      "label": "Background color"
     }
    ],
    "apiVersion": 1,
    "description": "Choose the timeline's colors and fonts: its labels, its measure (the axis, tick marks and their times), the halo that keeps them readable over images, the default marker and duration colors, and a background color. Set them here in the plugin list; anything left blank keeps the usual look, and colors chosen for events still win."
   },
   "settings": {
    "textFont": "Georgia, serif",
    "haloColor": "#0b1733",
    "textColor": "#f3d27a",
    "markerColor": "#b3122e",
    "measureColor": "#e8c25a",
    "durationColor": "#c8102e",
    "backgroundColor": "#0b1733"
   }
  }
 ],
 "tags": [
  "history",
  "military history",
  "united states marine corps",
  "education"
 ],
 "image": {
  "alt": "Joe Rosenthal's \"Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima\", 23 February 1945",
  "url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger_-_edit1.jpg/1280px-Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger_-_edit1.jpg"
 },
 "events": [
  {
   "id": "abbey-gate",
   "time": "1629936000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "afghanistan",
     "memorial"
    ],
    "title": "Abbey Gate bombing at Kabul airport",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/2021_Kabul_airport_bombing_explosion_%2802%29.png/960px-2021_Kabul_airport_bombing_explosion_%2802%29.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Kabul_airport_attack"
    ],
    "description": "An ISIS-K suicide bomber struck the crowds at Abbey Gate during the evacuation, killing **13 American service members** (11 of them Marines) and about 170 Afghans.\n\n**Read more:** [2021 Kabul airport attack — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Kabul_airport_attack)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "act-1798",
   "time": "-5411232000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "founding",
     "law"
    ],
    "title": "President John Adams signs the act establishing the Marine Corps",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/John_Adams_Portrait.jpg/960px-John_Adams_Portrait.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Adams"
    ],
    "description": "\"An Act for the establishing and organizing a Marine Corps\" created the **United States Marine Corps** as a permanent service, at first under the Navy at sea and liable for duty on land at the President's direction.\n\n**Read more:** [John Adams — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Adams)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "al-gray",
   "time": "552096000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "commandant",
     "doctrine"
    ],
    "title": "Al Gray becomes Commandant",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Official_Portrait_of_Retired_Gen._Alfred_M._Gray_%282%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Official_Portrait_of_Retired_Gen._Alfred_M._Gray_%282%29_%28cropped%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_M._Gray_Jr."
    ],
    "description": "A former enlisted Marine, the 29th Commandant made **maneuver warfare** the Corps' doctrine, published in *FMFM 1, Warfighting* (1989), and insisted that \"every Marine is a rifleman\".\n\n**Read more:** [Alfred M. Gray Jr. — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_M._Gray_Jr.)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "armistice",
   "time": "-1613865600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "treaty",
     "world war i"
    ],
    "title": "Armistice: the guns fall silent",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Armisticetrain_%28slight_crop%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armistice_of_11_November_1918"
    ],
    "description": "The armistice took effect at 11 a.m. Marines were still fighting that morning, crossing the Meuse River the night before. Over 2,400 Marines were killed in the war.\n\n**Read more:** [Armistice of 11 November 1918 — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armistice_of_11_November_1918)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "armistice-korea",
   "time": "-518572800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "treaty",
     "korean war"
    ],
    "title": "Korean Armistice Agreement",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Korean_War_armistice_agreement_1953.jpg/960px-Korean_War_armistice_agreement_1953.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Armistice_Agreement"
    ],
    "description": "The armistice ended the fighting, though no peace treaty has ever been signed. About 4,500 Marines died in Korea.\n\n**Read more:** [Korean Armistice Agreement — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Armistice_Agreement)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "aviation",
   "time": "-1818115200/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "aviation",
     "founding"
    ],
    "title": "Birth of Marine Corps aviation",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/USMC-19400.jpg/960px-USMC-19400.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_A._Cunningham"
    ],
    "description": "First Lieutenant **Alfred A. Cunningham** reported for flight training at Annapolis, becoming the first Marine aviator. The day is celebrated as the birthday of Marine aviation.\n\n**Read more:** [Alfred Austell Cunningham — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_A._Cunningham)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "barracks",
   "time": "-5325436800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "tradition",
     "place"
    ],
    "title": "Marine Barracks Washington: \"8th & I\"",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/U.S._Marines_pass_in_review_at_the_closing_the_retirement_ceremony_for_Gen._George_J._Flynn%2C_not_shown_at_Marine_Barracks_Washington_in_Washington%2C_D.C.%2C_May_9%2C_2013_130509-M-LU710-372.jpg/960px-thumbnail.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Barracks,_Washington,_D.C."
    ],
    "description": "President Thomas Jefferson and Commandant Burrows chose the site of the Marine Barracks near the Washington Navy Yard, by tradition while riding together on this day. \"8th & I\" is the **oldest post of the Corps** and the Commandant's home since 1806.\n\n**Read more:** [Marine Barracks, Washington, D.C. — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_Barracks,_Washington,_D.C.)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "basilone",
   "time": "-858038400/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "medal of honor",
     "guadalcanal"
    ],
    "title": "John Basilone at Henderson Field",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/John_Basilone%2C_September_1943_%2810931006014%29.jpg/960px-John_Basilone%2C_September_1943_%2810931006014%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Basilone"
    ],
    "description": "Sergeant \"Manila John\" Basilone's machine-gun section held through two nights of attacks, Basilone repairing guns and running ammunition through enemy lines. Awarded the **Medal of Honor**, he insisted on returning to combat and was killed on the first day at Iwo Jima, receiving the Navy Cross.\n\n**Read more:** [John Basilone — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Basilone)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "beirut",
   "time": "435715200/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "lebanon",
     "memorial"
    ],
    "title": "Beirut barracks bombing",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Beirutbarr.jpg/960px-Beirutbarr.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1983_Beirut_barracks_bombings"
    ],
    "description": "A truck bomb destroyed the headquarters of the 1st Battalion, 8th Marines at Beirut airport, killing **241 American servicemen**, 220 of them Marines: the Corps' deadliest single day since Iwo Jima. A second bomb killed 58 French paratroopers.\n\n**Read more:** [1983 Beirut barracks bombings — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1983_Beirut_barracks_bombings)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "birthday",
   "time": "-6126537600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "founding",
     "tradition"
    ],
    "title": "The Continental Congress raises two battalions of Marines",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Sketch_of_Tun_Tavern_in_the_Revolutionary_War.jpg/960px-Sketch_of_Tun_Tavern_in_the_Revolutionary_War.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tun_Tavern"
    ],
    "description": "The Second Continental Congress resolved \"that two Battalions of Marines be raised\", men \"good seamen, or so acquainted with maritime affairs as to be able to serve to advantage by sea\".\n\nTradition places the first recruiting at **Tun Tavern** in Philadelphia. Marines celebrate **10 November** as the Corps' birthday every year.\n\n**Read more:** [Tun Tavern — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tun_Tavern)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "birthday-order",
   "time": "-1520035200/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "tradition",
     "founding"
    ],
    "title": "Lejeune's birthday message (Marine Corps Order No. 47)",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/MarineCorpsBirthdayCake_CampLejeune_2008_081107-M-3189M-001.jpg/960px-MarineCorpsBirthdayCake_CampLejeune_2008_081107-M-3189M-001.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Marine_Corps_Birthday"
    ],
    "description": "Lejeune ordered that a summary of the Corps' history and traditions be read to every command each **10 November**. The message is still read at every Marine Corps birthday ball.\n\n**Read more:** [United States Marine Corps birthday — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Marine_Corps_Birthday)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bladensburg",
   "time": "-4902595200/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "war of 1812"
    ],
    "title": "Battle of Bladensburg",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Final_Stand_at_Bladensburg.png/960px-Final_Stand_at_Bladensburg.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bladensburg"
    ],
    "description": "As American militia broke, about 100 Marines and 400 sailors under Commodore Joshua Barney held their line until overrun, earning the respect of the British. Washington was burned that night, but the Marine Barracks and Commandant's House were spared.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Bladensburg — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bladensburg)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "boyington",
   "time": "-820368000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "aviation",
     "medal of honor"
    ],
    "title": "Pappy Boyington is shot down",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Pappy_Boyington.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pappy_Boyington"
    ],
    "description": "Commander of VMF-214, the **\"Black Sheep\" squadron**, Boyington was credited with 26 victories (28 by his own count) before being shot down over Rabaul and captured. He spent the rest of the war as a prisoner and received the **Medal of Honor**.\n\n**Read more:** [Gregory Boyington — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pappy_Boyington)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "brewer",
   "time": "263692800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "women marines",
     "milestone"
    ],
    "title": "Margaret A. Brewer: the first woman general",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Brewer_MA_USMC.jpg/960px-Brewer_MA_USMC.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_A._Brewer"
    ],
    "description": "The last Director of Women Marines became the first woman in the Marine Corps to reach general officer rank, as brigadier general and Director of Information.\n\n**Read more:** [Margaret A. Brewer — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_A._Brewer)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bull-run",
   "time": "-3422304000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "civil war"
    ],
    "title": "First Battle of Bull Run",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Bridge_during_the_Retreat_from_Manassas%2C_First_Battle_of_Bull_Run_%28First_Manassas%29%2C_Virginia_1861.jpg/960px-Bridge_during_the_Retreat_from_Manassas%2C_First_Battle_of_Bull_Run_%28First_Manassas%29%2C_Virginia_1861.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Battle_of_Bull_Run"
    ],
    "description": "A battalion of mostly raw Marine recruits fought with the Union army in the first major battle of the war and was swept up in its retreat: one of the Corps' few battles ending in defeat.\n\n**Read more:** [First Battle of Bull Run — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Battle_of_Bull_Run)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "burrows",
   "time": "-5411145600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "commandant"
    ],
    "title": "William Ward Burrows becomes Commandant",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/William_Burrows_MET_APS6820.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Ward_Burrows_II"
    ],
    "description": "The first Commandant of the new Marine Corps. Burrows founded the **Marine Band** in 1798 and moved the Corps' headquarters to Washington.\n\n**Read more:** [William Ward Burrows II — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Ward_Burrows_II)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "camp-rhino",
   "time": "1006646400/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "afghanistan",
     "amphibious"
    ],
    "title": "Task Force 58 seizes Camp Rhino",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Flag_of_the_United_States_Marine_Corps.svg/960px-Flag_of_the_United_States_Marine_Corps.svg.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_Rhino"
    ],
    "description": "Marines under Brigadier General **James Mattis** flew some 400 miles inland from ships in the Arabian Sea to seize a desert airstrip near Kandahar: the first conventional US ground forces in Afghanistan and one of the deepest amphibious operations in history.\n\n**Read more:** [Camp Rhino — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_Rhino)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "carpenter",
   "time": "1290297600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "medal of honor",
     "afghanistan"
    ],
    "title": "Kyle Carpenter covers a grenade in Marjah",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Kyle_Carpenter_140618-M-LI307-0155.jpg/960px-Kyle_Carpenter_140618-M-LI307-0155.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyle_Carpenter"
    ],
    "description": "Lance Corporal Carpenter shielded a fellow Marine from a grenade on a rooftop in Helmand, suffering grave wounds that took dozens of surgeries to repair. He is the youngest living recipient of the **Medal of Honor**.\n\n**Read more:** [Kyle Carpenter — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kyle_Carpenter)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "chapultepec",
   "time": "-3859488000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "mexican–american war",
     "tradition"
    ],
    "title": "Storming of Chapultepec: \"the Halls of Montezuma\"",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Nebel_Mexican_War_10_Chapultepec_Pillow_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Nebel_Mexican_War_10_Chapultepec_Pillow_%28cropped%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Chapultepec"
    ],
    "description": "Marines joined the assault on the castle guarding Mexico City, then entered the city and guarded the National Palace.\n\nThe Marines' Hymn opens with **\"From the Halls of Montezuma\"**, and a popular tradition links the scarlet **\"blood stripe\"** on dress blue trousers to the officers and NCOs who fell here, though the stripe predates the war.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Chapultepec — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Chapultepec)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "chesty-puller",
   "time": "-857952000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "navy cross"
    ],
    "title": "Lewis \"Chesty\" Puller",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Chesty-puller.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chesty_Puller"
    ],
    "description": "The most decorated Marine in history, with **five Navy Crosses**: two from Nicaragua, one for his battalion's stand at Henderson Field, one at Cape Gloucester and one at Chosin. Recruits still end the day with *\"Good night, Chesty, wherever you are!\"*\n\n**Read more:** [Chesty Puller — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chesty_Puller)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "code-talkers",
   "time": "-784684800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "milestone",
     "code talkers"
    ],
    "title": "Navajo Code Talkers at Iwo Jima",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Navajo_code_talkers_%284374070382%29.jpg/960px-Navajo_code_talkers_%284374070382%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_talker",
     "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navajo_code_talkers_(4374070382).jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Navajo Marines transmitted messages in a code built on their language that the Japanese never broke. At Iwo Jima six code talkers sent and received more than 800 messages without error in the first two days.\n\n> \"Were it not for the Navajos, the Marines would never have taken Iwo Jima.\" — Major Howard Connor, 5th Marine Division signal officer\n\n**Read more:** [Code talker — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_talker)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons ([file page](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navajo_code_talkers_(4374070382).jpg)).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "corregidor",
   "time": "-872812800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "world war ii"
    ],
    "title": "The 4th Marines at Corregidor",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/American_and_Filipino_Prisoners%2C_Captured_at_Corregidor_-_NARA_-_6200939.jpg/960px-American_and_Filipino_Prisoners%2C_Captured_at_Corregidor_-_NARA_-_6200939.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Corregidor"
    ],
    "description": "The 4th Marine Regiment, withdrawn from Shanghai to the Philippines, defended the beaches of Corregidor until the island surrendered. Colonel Samuel Howard ordered the regiment's colors burned rather than surrendered; its survivors spent the rest of the war as prisoners.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Corregidor — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Corregidor)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "da-nang",
   "time": "-152064000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "vietnam war",
     "amphibious"
    ],
    "title": "Marines land at Da Nang",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Beach_activity_at_Da_Nang%2C_Vietnam_during_landing_of_United_States_Marines_of_the_9th_Marine_Expeditionary_Brigade_in_March_of_1965.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9th_Marine_Expeditionary_Brigade"
    ],
    "description": "The 9th Marine Expeditionary Brigade came ashore to guard the Da Nang air base: the first American ground combat units in South Vietnam.\n\n**Read more:** [9th Marine Expeditionary Brigade — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9th_Marine_Expeditionary_Brigade)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "dan-daly",
   "time": "-1627516800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "medal of honor",
     "world war i"
    ],
    "title": "Dan Daly: \"Do you want to live forever?\"",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/DanielDaly.jpg/960px-DanielDaly.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Daly"
    ],
    "description": "One of only two Marines to receive **two Medals of Honor** for separate actions (Peking 1900 and Haiti 1915). At Belleau Wood, Gunnery Sergeant Daly is said to have led a charge with the cry *\"Come on, you sons of bitches, do you want to live forever?\"*\n\n**Read more:** [Daniel Daly — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Daly)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "derna",
   "time": "-5196873600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "barbary"
    ],
    "title": "Battle of Derna: \"to the shores of Tripoli\"",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Attack_on_Derna_by_Charles_Waterhouse_01.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Derna_(1805)"
    ],
    "description": "After a 500-mile march across the Libyan desert from Egypt, Lieutenant **Presley O'Bannon**, eight Marines and a force of mercenaries took the port of Derna. It was the first time the US flag was raised in victory on foreign soil.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Derna (1805) — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Derna_(1805))\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "douglas-mansfield",
   "time": "-552614400/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "law",
     "organization"
    ],
    "title": "The Douglas–Mansfield Act",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/CAC_CC_001_18_29_0000_2899_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-CAC_CC_001_18_29_0000_2899_%28cropped%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Mansfield"
    ],
    "description": "Congress fixed the Marine Corps' strength at no fewer than **three divisions and three air wings**, and gave the Commandant a voice on the Joint Chiefs of Staff on matters concerning the Corps.\n\nBoth sponsors were former Marines: Senator **Paul Douglas** enlisted as a private at 50 and was wounded at Peleliu and Okinawa; Representative **Mike Mansfield** had served as a Marine private in the Philippines and China.\n\n**Read more:** [Mike Mansfield — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Mansfield)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "dunham",
   "time": "1081900800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "medal of honor",
     "iraq"
    ],
    "title": "Jason Dunham covers a grenade at Karabilah",
    "iconUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/JasonDunham.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jason_Dunham"
    ],
    "description": "Corporal Dunham threw himself on a grenade with his Kevlar helmet to shield two fellow Marines near the Syrian border, and died of his wounds eight days later. He was the first Marine to receive the **Medal of Honor** for Iraq.\n\n**Read more:** [Jason Dunham — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jason_Dunham)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "ega",
   "time": "-3190924800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "tradition",
     "emblem"
    ],
    "title": "The Eagle, Globe and Anchor is adopted",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Eagle%2C_Globe_and_Anchor.svg/960px-Eagle%2C_Globe_and_Anchor.svg.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle,_Globe,_and_Anchor"
    ],
    "description": "Commandant **Jacob Zeilin** approved the emblem: an eagle for the nation, a globe for worldwide service and a fouled anchor for the Corps' naval tradition. A version of it is still the Marine Corps emblem.\n\n**Read more:** [Eagle, Globe, and Anchor — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle,_Globe,_and_Anchor)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "evans-carlson",
   "time": "-863913600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "leader",
     "raiders",
     "tradition"
    ],
    "title": "Evans Carlson and \"Gung Ho\"",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Colonel_Evans_F._Carlson_Trim_Edit.jpg/960px-Colonel_Evans_F._Carlson_Trim_Edit.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evans_Carlson"
    ],
    "description": "Carlson had observed Mao's Eighth Route Army in China, and brought back its spirit of shared hardship and the Chinese phrase *gōng hé*, \"work together\", as his Raiders' motto. \"Gung Ho\" passed into the Corps, and into English.\n\n**Read more:** [Evans Carlson — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evans_Carlson)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "firdos",
   "time": "1049846400/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "iraq",
     "iconic"
    ],
    "title": "The Firdos Square statue falls",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/SaddamStatue.jpg/960px-SaddamStatue.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saddam_Hussein_statue_destruction"
    ],
    "description": "Marines of the 3rd Battalion, 4th Marines helped Iraqis pull down a statue of Saddam Hussein in central Baghdad, televised around the world as the symbolic fall of the regime.\n\n**Read more:** [Firdos Square statue destruction — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saddam_Hussein_statue_destruction)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "flag-raising",
   "time": "-784339200/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "world war ii",
     "iconic"
    ],
    "title": "Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger_-_edit1.jpg/960px-Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger_-_edit1.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima"
    ],
    "description": "Joe Rosenthal's photograph of six men raising the second, larger flag atop Mount Suribachi became one of the most reproduced images in history and won the Pulitzer Prize. Three of the six were killed in the battle that followed.\n\nThe photograph is the model for the **Marine Corps War Memorial** in Arlington, Virginia, dedicated in 1954.\n\n**Read more:** [Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "fmf",
   "time": "-1138233600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "doctrine",
     "organization",
     "amphibious"
    ],
    "title": "The Fleet Marine Force is established",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Seal_of_the_United_States_Marine_Corps.svg/960px-Seal_of_the_United_States_Marine_Corps.svg.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleet_Marine_Force"
    ],
    "description": "The Navy Department created the Fleet Marine Force as a standing amphibious assault force of the fleet. The next year Marine officers at Quantico produced the *Tentative Manual for Landing Operations*, the basis of the amphibious doctrine used in World War II.\n\n**Read more:** [Fleet Marine Force — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleet_Marine_Force)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "force-design",
   "time": "1583020800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "doctrine",
     "organization"
    ],
    "title": "Force Design 2030",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Gen._David_H._Berger.jpg/960px-Gen._David_H._Berger.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Force_Design_2030",
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Berger_(general)"
    ],
    "description": "Commandant **David H. Berger** announced a sweeping redesign to fight a peer adversary in the Indo-Pacific: tanks divested, artillery cut and new **Marine Littoral Regiments** with long-range missiles. The plan was debated fiercely, including by retired generals. *(Published in March 2020; the day here marks the month.)*\n\n**Read more:** [Force Design 2030 — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Force_Design_2030)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons ([file page](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Berger_(general))).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "fort-fisher",
   "time": "-3312230400/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "civil war",
     "amphibious"
    ],
    "title": "Second Battle of Fort Fisher",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Assault_and_capture_of_Fort_Fisher_January_1865_Harper%27s_Weekly.png/960px-Assault_and_capture_of_Fort_Fisher_January_1865_Harper%27s_Weekly.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Battle_of_Fort_Fisher"
    ],
    "description": "Marines and sailors assaulted the sea face of the fort guarding Wilmington, the Confederacy's last major port, while Army troops attacked from the land side. The fort fell that night. Several Marines received the Medal of Honor.\n\n**Read more:** [Second Battle of Fort Fisher — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Battle_of_Fort_Fisher)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "fort-riviere",
   "time": "-1708041600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "banana wars"
    ],
    "title": "Battle of Fort Rivière",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Fort_Rivi%C3%A8re%2C_Haiti%2C_after_its_capture_by_US_Marine_Corps_and_Navy_personnel%2C_1915.png/960px-Fort_Rivi%C3%A8re%2C_Haiti%2C_after_its_capture_by_US_Marine_Corps_and_Navy_personnel%2C_1915.png",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fort_Rivi%C3%A8re"
    ],
    "description": "The decisive battle of the First Caco War in Haiti. A small force of Marines and sailors broke into the old French fort through a drain and took it in minutes.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Fort Rivière — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fort_Rivi%C3%A8re)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "frequent-wind",
   "time": "167961600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "evacuation",
     "vietnam war"
    ],
    "title": "Operation Frequent Wind: the fall of Saigon",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/South_Vietnamese_helicopter_is_pushed_over_the_side_of_the_USS_Okinawa_during_Operation_Frequent_Wind%2C_April_1975.jpg/960px-South_Vietnamese_helicopter_is_pushed_over_the_side_of_the_USS_Okinawa_during_Operation_Frequent_Wind%2C_April_1975.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Frequent_Wind"
    ],
    "description": "Marine and Air Force helicopters lifted more than **7,000 Americans and Vietnamese** from Saigon to ships offshore in about 18 hours. The last Marines left the embassy roof on the morning of 30 April.\n\n**Read more:** [Operation Frequent Wind — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Frequent_Wind)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "grenada",
   "time": "435888000/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "grenada",
     "amphibious"
    ],
    "title": "Operation Urgent Fury: Grenada",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/CH-53D_HMM-261_Grenada_Okt1983.jpeg/960px-CH-53D_HMM-261_Grenada_Okt1983.jpeg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_invasion_of_Grenada"
    ],
    "description": "Two days after the Beirut bombing, Marines of the 22nd Marine Amphibious Unit, diverted on their way to Lebanon, landed in the north of Grenada and then at Grand Mal, alongside Army Rangers and paratroopers.\n\n**Read more:** [United States invasion of Grenada — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_invasion_of_Grenada)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "guadalcanal-end",
   "time": "-848707200/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "event",
     "world war ii",
     "guadalcanal"
    ],
    "title": "Guadalcanal secured",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Marines_rest_in_the_field_on_Guadalcanal.jpg/960px-Marines_rest_in_the_field_on_Guadalcanal.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalcanal_campaign"
    ],
    "description": "The last Japanese troops were evacuated from the island. The 1st Marine Division, relieved in December, received a **Presidential Unit Citation**; Vandegrift received the Medal of Honor.\n\n**Read more:** [Guadalcanal campaign — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalcanal_campaign)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "guantanamo",
   "time": "-2258236800/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "spanish–american war",
     "amphibious"
    ],
    "title": "Marines land at Guantánamo Bay",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/LC-USZC4-2678_%2817522430051%29.jpg/960px-LC-USZC4-2678_%2817522430051%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Guant%C3%A1namo_Bay"
    ],
    "description": "The 1st Marine Battalion under Lieutenant Colonel Robert Huntington seized the bay as a coaling station for the fleet blockading Santiago, and defended it in several days of fighting, including at Cuzco Well on 14 June.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Guantánamo Bay — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Guant%C3%A1namo_Bay)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (from the article above).*"
   }
