     "https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/valor-the-only-way-out/"
    ],
    "description": "Engineers cut an airstrip out of the frozen ground at Hagaru-ri, working through the nights under fire. It opened on 1 December, and over about six days Air Force C-47s flew out **4,608 wounded** and 81 dead.\n\n**Read more:** [Battle of Chosin Reservoir — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Chosin_Reservoir); [Valor: The Only Way Out — Air & Space Forces Magazine](https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/valor-the-only-way-out/)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (map of Hagaru-ri, 1–4 December 1950, from the Marine Corps' official history).*"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "koto-ri-burial",
   "time": "-601689600/1",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "battle",
     "korean war",
     "chosin",
     "memorial"
    ],
    "title": "117 dead are buried at Koto-ri",
    "iconUrl": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Marines_Move_Out_of_Koto-ri%2C_8_December_1950_%2815752476789%29.jpg/960px-Marines_Move_Out_of_Koto-ri%2C_8_December_1950_%2815752476789%29.jpg",
    "sources": [
     "https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/chosin-battle"
    ],
    "description": "With the ground frozen and the road south still to be fought for, **117 Marines, soldiers and Royal Marines commandos** were buried together in one grave at Koto-ri on 8 December 1950, before the division moved out.\n\n**Read more:** [The Battle of Chosin — PBS American Experience](https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/chosin-battle)\n\n*Image: Wikimedia Commons (Marines move out of Koto-ri, 8 December 1950; USMC Archives, CC BY 2.0).*"
   }
  },
  {
